d 80. Therefore, contradiction, which means somewhere my assumption is wrong.\n\nHmm, so there's a flaw in the reasoning. Let me check.\n\nFrom the first meeting, the distance covered by wagon A in two legs: first 50 km to the first meeting, then from the first meeting to the second. Let me call this distance x. Similarly, wagon B covers y from first meeting to second.\n\nTotal x + y = D. From the speed ratio, during t2, the time between first and second meeting:\n\nWagon A's distance: x = 50 * (v1/v2) = 50 * (D - 50)/50 = (D - 50)\n\nWait, no. The ratio of speeds is v1/v2 = 50/(D - 50). So in time t2, the distance covered by wagon A is v1 * t2 = 50/(D - 50) * v2 * t2 = (50 / (D - 50)) * (D - 50) * t2 / t2 )??? Not sure.\n\nWait, to elaborate, if from first to second meeting, the time is t2. Then the distance covered by wagon A is v1 * t2 = [50 / t1] * t2 = 50 * (t2 / t1). Similarly, wagon B's distance is v2 * t2 = [ (D - 50) / t1 ] * t2 = (D - 50) * (t2 / t1). Since t1 = 50 / v1 = (D - 50) / v2, so t1 = 50 / v1 = (D - 50) / v2 => v1 / v2 = 50 / (D - 50) => v1 = [50 / (D - 50)] * v2. Then, substituting into the distances:\n\ndistance_A = v1 * t2 = [50 / (D - 50)] * v2 * t2\n\ndistance_B = v2 * t2\n\nBut since t2 / t1 = t2 / [50 / v1] = [v1 / 50] * t2. But v1 = [50 / (D - 50)] * v2, so:\n\nt2 / t1 = [50 / (D - 50)] * t2 / 50 = t2 / (D - 50 )\n\nHence, distance_A = [50 / (D - 50)] * v2 * t2\n\ndistance_B = v2 * t2\n\nTherefore, distance_A / distance_B = 50 / (D - 50)\n\nBut we also know that distance_A + distance_B = D.\n\nTherefore:\n\ndistance_B [ (50 / (D - 50)) + 1 ] = D\n\n=> distance_B [ (50 + D - 50 ) / (D - 50) ) ] = D\n\n=> distance_B * ( D / (D - 50) ) = D\n\n=> distance_B = D - 50\n\nHence, since distance_B = v2 * t2 = D - 50\n\nSimilarly, distance_A = [50 / (D - 50)] * v2 * t2 = [50 / (D - 50)] * (D - 50) = 50\n\nTherefore, distance_A = 50 km, distance_B = D - 50 km\n\nBut wait, distance_A is the distance covered by wagon A from first to second meeting. Bu