uld imply the total distance D = D + 30, which is impossible. So, this line of thinking is wrong.\n\nAlternative idea: After the first meeting, the two wagons have swapped directions. The time taken for the first meeting is t1, and the time taken between the first and second meeting is t2. We know:\n\nFrom the first meeting to the second:\n\nWagon A starts at distance x from A (50 km), turns around, and meets B at a point 30 km from B. The key is to compute the distance each travels in t2.\n\nIf the speed ratio remains v1/v2 = 50/(D - 50). In time t2, the distance covered by A is (50/(D - 50)) * v2 t2.\n\nBut total distance covered in the second phase is D (since they start from opposite ends and meet after covering the entire distance between them).\n\nWait, no. In the second phase, from first to second meeting, the distance between them is D. Since they are moving towards each other, the time taken is t2 = D/(v1 + v2). So, during this time, the total distance covered by both is D, so:\n\nv1 t2 + v2 t2 = D\n\nBut we can also calculate this as (v1/v2) * v2 t2 + v2 t2 = ( (v1/v2) + 1 ) * v2 t2 = D\n\nSince v1 / v2 = 50/(D - 50), then:\n\n(50/(D - 50) + 1 ) * v2 t2 = D\n\n=> ( (50 + D - 50 ) / (D - 50) ) * v2 t2 = D\n\n=> ( D / (D - 50) ) * v2 t2 = D\n\n=> v2 t2 = D - 50\n\nBut from the first meeting, we have:\n\nv2 t1 = D - 50\n\nAnd t1 = (D - 50)/v2, so indeed.\n\nBut since the total distance covered in time t2 is D, then as above:\n\n( D / (D - 50) ) * v2 t2 = D => gives v2 t2 = D - 50. So that's consistent.\n\nBut then, the distance covered by wagon A in time t2 is:\n\ndistance_A = v1 t2 = [50/(D - 50)] * v2 t2 = [50/(D - 50)]*(D - 50) = 50 km\n\nSimilarly, distance_B = v2 t2 = D - 50 km.\n\nSo, the first part of the journey, wagon A traveled 50 km to the first meeting point, then in the second phase, traveled 50 km again. So total of 50 + 50 = 100 km.\n\nSimilarly, wagon B traveled D - 50 km to the first meeting point, then D - 50 km again, totaling 2*(D - 50) km