erefore, their relative speed is D / t2 = (v1 + v2). Hence, t2 = D / (v1 + v2). Which matches.\n\nOkay, so we have:\n\nt2 = D / (v1 + v2 )\n\nBut also from the first meeting:\n\nt1 = 50 / v1 = (D - 50)/v2\n\nTherefore, v1 = 50 v2 / (D - 50 )\n\nSubstituting into expression for t2:\n\nt2 = D / (v1 + v2 ) = D / ( (50v2)/(D - 50 ) + v2 ) = D / [ v2 (50/(D - 50 ) + 1 ) ] = D / [ v2 (50 + D - 50 ) / (D - 50 ) ] = D / [ v2 (D ) / (D - 50 ) ] = (D - 50 ) / (v2 D )\n\nBut we also know that t2 = D / (v1 + v2 ) = (D ) / ( (50v2 / (D - 50 ) ) + v2 ) = (D ) / [ v2 (50 / (D - 50 ) + 1 ) ] = (D ) / [ v2 (50 + D - 50 ) / (D - 50 ) ) ] = (D ) / [ v2 ( D ) / (D - 50 ) ) ] = (D - 50 ) / (v2 D )\n\nHence:\n\nFrom t2: D / (v1 + v2 ) = (D - 50 ) / (v2 D )\n\nMultiply both sides by (v1 + v2 ) v2 D:\n\nThis gives:\n\nD * v2 D = (D - 50 ) (v1 + v2 )\n\nBut v1 = 50 v2 / ( D - 50 )\n\nSo substitute:\n\nLeft side: D^2 v2\n\nRight side: (D - 50 ) [ 50 v2 / ( D - 50 ) + v2 ] = (D - 50 ) [ (50 v2 + v2 D - 50 v2 ) / ( D - 50 ) ) ] = (D - 50 ) [ v2 D / ( D - 50 ) ) ] = v2 D\n\nTherefore, equate left and right:\n\nD^2 v2 = v2 D\n\nAssuming v2 ≠ 0, divide both sides by v2:\n\nD^2 = D => D = 1. But this is impossible.\n\nWait, so where's the mistake here?\n\nWait, substitution may have been wrong. Let's take:\n\nFrom first meeting: v1 = 50 v2 / (D - 50 )\n\nSubstituting into the equation for t2:\n\nt2 = (D - 50 ) / (v2 D )\n\nBut from the first meeting, t1 = (D - 50 ) / v2\n\nTherefore, total time until the second meeting: t1 + t2 = D / v1 + t2 = D / v1 + (D - 50 ) / v2\n\nBut t1 + t2 should equal D / v1 (from the first meeting time). Wait, is this assumption correct?\n\nWait, actually, from the first meeting until the second meeting, time t2. But starting from the start until the first meeting is t1. So total time until the second meeting is t1 + t2.\n\nHowever, the total time from start to second meeting should also be equal to D / v1 (since the wagon A, starting from A, takes time D / v1 to reach 